WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 185: Richard Nixon: The Checkers Speech, the Kitchen Debate, and the Opening to China (1950–1972)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.360
[Rebecca] Today we step into the extraordinary Shakespearean saga of Richard Nixon.

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:22.960
[Rebecca] Before Watergate cast its dark shadow, Nixon was a master of political survival and grand international strategy, whose career was marked by iconic television moments and historic diplomacy.

5
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:31.760
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Checker's speech, the kitchen debate, and the historic flight to Beijing.

6
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:42.400
[George] Richard Milhis Nixon was born in January 1913 in a drafty wooden house his father built beside a struggling lemon orchard in Yorbalinda, California.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:49.440
[George] His upbringing was steeped in strict Quaker piety, relentless manual labor, and profound family tragedy.

8
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:57.680
[George] Two of his brothers died young of tuberculosis, leaving an indelible imprint of solemn reserve and insecurity upon the sensitive boy.

9
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:07.280
[George] Working long hours at his father's combination grocery store and gas station in Whittier, young Dick excelled through sheer, ferocious discipline.

10
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:17.360
[George] Graduating third in his class from Duke University School of Law, Nixon served in the South Pacific during World War II as a naval operations officer.

11
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:34.080
[George] Operating a hamburger and poker shack on Green Island nicknamed Nick's Snack Shack, the quiet Quaker officer displayed a photographic memory for cards, amassing $10,000 in poker winnings that he used to finance his first campaign for Congress in 1946.

12
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:47.520
[George] Defeating entrenched Democratic incumbent Jerry Vorheis and later Helen Gahagan Douglas, 37-year-old Nixon vaulted to national fame as the relentless investigator who exposed Soviet spy Alger Hiss.

13
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.720
[George] Winning a seat in the United States Senate and positioning himself as the premier anti-communist crusader in America.

14
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.840
[George] His remarkable intensity and tactical brilliance drew the attention of General Dwight D.

15
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.840
[George] Eisenhower, who tapped the young Californian as his vice presidential running mate in 1952.

16
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:19.480
[George] Those grueling poker games in the Pacific taught Nixon the vital art of psychological bluffing and risk assessment, skills he would later deploy against Soviet and Chinese communist leaders on the global stage.

17
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:30.120
[George] His rise from humble origins in Yorba Linda proved that in the American Republic, sheer determination and intellect could overcome any obstacle of social station or wealth.

18
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:39.800
[George] In September 1952, just two months before the presidential election, Nixon's political career was on the verge of sudden, ignominious death.

19
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:51.000
[George] The New York Post published a sensational front-page story accusing him of maintaining a secret $18,000 financial, slush fund provided by wealthy California donors.

20
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.760
[George] Eisenhower's senior advisors panicked, urging Ike to dump Nixon from the ticket immediately.

21
00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:05.000
[George] Desperate to defend his integrity, 39-year-old Nixon bought a half hour of national television time on September 23.

22
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.520
[George] Speaking live from an L.

23
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:29.080
[George] Capitan Theater studio in Hollywood to 60 million viewers, the largest television audience assembled in history up to that night, Nixon laid bare his family's personal finances with excruciating, emotional intimacy, his mortgages, his debts, his $4,000 life insurance policy, and his wife Pat's respectable Republican cloth coat.

24
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.400
[George] Then came the stroke of populist genius that entered American folklore.

25
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:41.720
[George] Nixon confessed that a supporter in Texas had sent his six-year old daughter Trisha a little black and white cocker spaniel puppy in a crate.

26
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:47.680
[George] And you know what the kids, like all kids, loved the dog, and I just want to say this right now.

27
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:51.200
[George] That regardless of what they say about it, we're gonna keep it.

28
00:03:51.440 --> 00:04:02.480
[George] Over four million letters and telegrams poured into Republican headquarters demanding Nixon remain on the ticket, rescuing his career and demonstrating the raw emotional power of television.

29
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:14.240
[George] The checker's speech demonstrated that an embattled politician could bypass hostile newspaper editorial boards and appeal directly to the hearts of everyday citizens through the intimacy of home television.

30
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:26.240
[George] The broadcast proved that emotional authenticity could overcome elite skepticism, establishing a direct bond between Nixon and the millions of ordinary Americans who saw themselves in his struggles.

31
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:36.240
[George] As Eisenhower's vice president, Nixon traveled to 62 countries, transforming the office from a ceremonial ghost into a vital diplomatic engine.

32
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:48.000
[George] In July 1959, Nixon traveled to Moscow to open the American national exhibition in Sokolniki Park, designed to showcase capitalist consumer goods to the Soviet public.

33
00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:00.960
[George] Escorting Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev through a full-scale model of an affordable six-room American suburban ranch home, a spontaneous, fiery confrontation erupted in front of a modern kitchen display.

34
00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:10.480
[George] Khrushchev belligerently waved his hands, boasting that Soviet rockets were vastly superior and predicting that his grandchildren would live under communism.

35
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.240
[George] Nixon did not back down an inch, standing toe-to-toe with the volatile Soviet dictator.

36
00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:31.960
[George] Nixon jabbed his forefinger squarely into Khrushchev's chest, arguing with passionate fluency that true national greatness was measured not in nuclear missiles, but in the freedom of ordinary citizens to choose their own homes, washing machines, and color televisions.

37
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:49.400
[George] Color videotapes of the kitchen debate were broadcast simultaneously on American and Soviet television networks, captivating audiences worldwide and establishing Nixon as a formidable statesman capable of standing eye to eye with the most intimidating tyrant in the communist world.

38
00:05:49.400 --> 00:06:01.320
[George] The spontaneous clash in Sokolniki Park revealed Nixon's extraordinary ability to defend American free market democracy with passion, quick wit, and total lack of physical intimidation.

39
00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:11.480
[George] That fierce exchange over consumer freedom became an iconic symbol of the ideological contest between Western individual liberty and Soviet state regimentation.

40
00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:17.960
[George] Nixon's political demise appeared final in November 1960, when he was narrowly defeated by John F.

41
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.040
[George] Kennedy in the presidential race.

42
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:38.920
[George] Two years later, after losing the 1962 California gubernatorial election to Pat Brown, a bitter, sleep-deprived Nixon held a famous post-election press conference, lashing out at reporters, You won't have Nixon to kick around anymore, gentlemen, because this is my last press conference.

43
00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.120
[George] Most commentators wrote his political obituary.

44
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.480
[George] Yet Nixon embarked on the most astonishing, disciplined comeback in American history.

45
00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:57.880
[George] Moving to New York as a high powered corporate lawyer, Nixon spent six years traveling tirelessly across the country, campaigning for Republican congressional candidates.

46
00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:05.960
[George] Building loyal relationships with local party activists, and reinventing himself as a mature, unshakable elder statesman.

47
00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:15.280
[George] In 1968, as America was torn apart by urban riots, anti war protests, and the shocking assassinations of Martin Luther King J.R.

48
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.480
[George] and Robert F.

49
00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:27.520
[George] Kennedy, Nixon appealed to the silent majority, the millions of ordinary, forgotten, law-abiding working-class citizens who paid their taxes and loved their country.

50
00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:36.960
[George] Winning the Republican nomination, Nixon defeated Hubert Humphrey in November 1968, completing the greatest comeback in presidential history.

51
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:47.920
[George] His triumphant return from the political wilderness proved that resilience and unyielding determination are the ultimate assets of political survival in a turbulent democratic republic.

52
00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:58.240
[George] His comeback showed an indomitable fighting spirit, rebuilding his career through relentless discipline and an encyclopedic command of domestic and international politics.

53
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:12.400
[George] Upon entering the Oval Office, Nixon recognized a colossal geopolitical reality that previous administrations had ignored: the growing, bitter ideological schism between the Soviet Union and the People's Republic of China.

54
00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:23.520
[George] For 23 years, ever since Mao Zedong's Communist Revolution in 1949, the United States had refused to officially recognize or communicate with mainland China.

55
00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:34.400
[George] Partnering with his brilliant national security advisor, Henry Kissinger, Nixon conceived a breathtaking diplomatic masterstroke, playing the China card against Moscow.

56
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:50.160
[George] In July 1971, while on an official tour in Pakistan, Kissinger feigned a severe stomach illness, slipped away under cover of darkness, and boarded a secret Pakistan International Airlines flight to Beijing to arrange a presidential summit.

57
00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] On July 15, Nixon stunned the entire planet by announcing on live television that he had accepted an invitation to visit the People's Republic of China.

58
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:10.120
[George] The move fundamentally altered the global Cold War chessboard, giving Washington unprecedented diplomatic leverage over both communist superpowers simultaneously.

59
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:20.280
[George] The secret flight to Beijing caught the Soviet Union completely off guard, fundamentally reshaping the global Cold War balance of power in favor of the United States.

60
00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:29.000
[George] The opening to China fundamentally tilted the Cold War balance, forcing the Soviet leadership to seek arms reduction treaties with Washington.

61
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:38.600
[George] On February 21, 1972, the presidential jet spirit of 76 touched down on the snowdusted tarmac of Beijing Airport.

62
00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:43.160
[George] In a moment, watched live by hundreds of millions of viewers across the globe.

63
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:56.360
[George] President Nixon emerged from the aircraft and deliberately walked forward with his hand outstretched to grasp the hand of Chinese Premier Zhou Enlai, symbolically erasing the famous diplomatic snub of 1954.

64
00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.880
[George] When Secretary of State John Foster Dulles had refused to shake Zhou's hand at Geneva.

65
00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:12.760
[George] Within hours, Nixon was escorted to the forbidden enclave of Zhangnanhai to meet Chairman Mao Zedong in his bookline study, engaging in deep philosophical discourse on world power.

66
00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:28.040
[George] Nixon toasted Zhou Enlai with fiery Mao Tai liquor in the Great Hall of the People, hiked along the Great Wall of China, and signed the landmark Shanghai Communique, which laid the foundation for modern normalized relations between the two global giants.

67
00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:38.840
[George] It was a diplomatic feat that only an unimpeachable anti-communist like Nixon could have accomplished without facing charges of appeasement, giving rise to the immortal political adage.

68
00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.080
[George] Only Nixon could go to China.

69
00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:52.480
[George] Nixon's historic toasts in the Great Hall of the People and his meetings with Mao ended a quarter century of mutual isolation, opening China to global trade and modern international diplomacy.

70
00:10:52.480 --> 00:11:02.640
[George] The journey to Beijing transformed Nixon from a partisan fighter into a world historical statesman, forever changing the trajectory of global commerce and diplomacy.

71
00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:15.920
[George] Three months after his triumphant journey to China, Nixon flew to Moscow to sign the historic Strategic Arms Limitation Treaty, SALT I, and the Antiballistic Missile Treaty with Soviet Premier Leonid Brezhnev.

72
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.280
[George] Ushering in the era of détente, a peaceful relaxation of Cold War tensions.

73
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:26.880
[George] On the domestic front, Nixon presided over an astonishingly progressive legislative record.

74
00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:32.000
[George] He created the Environmental Protection Agency, EPA by executive order.

75
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:46.880
[George] Signed the Clean Air Act of 1970 and Clean Water Act of 1972, established the Occupational Safety and Health Administration OSHA, and signed Title IX, revolutionizing women's collegiate athletics forever.

76
00:11:46.880 --> 00:12:07.040
[George] In November 1972, running against liberal antiwar Democrat George McGovern, Nixon achieved one of the most lopsided landslides in the history of the Republic, winning 49 of 50 states, capturing 60.7% of the popular vote, and securing 520 electoral votes.

77
00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:14.800
[George] Standing at the absolute pinnacle of global acclaim and political dominion, Richard Nixon seemed destined for an immortal legacy.

78
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:20.480
[George] Yet beneath the golden surface of his triumph, the seeds of Watergate were already taking root.

79
00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:32.040
[George] His massive 49-state landslide represented the absolute summit of his political life, uniting a broad national consensus behind his foreign policy realism and domestic reforms.

80
00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:42.200
[George] His domestic environmental and educational legacy continues to protect the air, water, and civil rights of millions of Americans across generations.

81
00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:55.800
[Rebecca] Richard Nixon achieved one of the most stunning political resurrections in history, rewriting the global Cold War order with his historic journey to Beijing and winning 49 states in 1972.

82
00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:04.120
[Rebecca] But the very paranoia and ruthless siege mentality that fueled his ascent would ultimately engineer his catastrophic destruction.

83
00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:18.200
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the darkest political drama in American history: Watergate, the secret Oval Office Ting System, the Saturday Night Massacre, the Smoking Gun Tape, and the tragic resignation of Richard Nixon.

84
00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:22.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

